{"step_index":138,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-24T17:16:37Z","input_tokens":2536,"cache_read_tokens":78741,"output_tokens":1400,"content":"### 1. Tooling & Environment Verification\n\nAll required tools are in place:\n\n| Component | Status | Details |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Python Environment** | ✅ Ready | Python 3.12.13 with active virtual environment |\n| **Data & Relational Engines** | ✅ Ready | `pandas` (3.0.5) for tabular data, built-in `sqlite3` for SQL snapshot query execution |\n| **Document Parsers** | ✅ Ready | `/usr/bin/pdftotext` (system binary) & `pypdf` (installed) for PDF document parsing |\n| **Data Schema & Serialization** | ✅ Ready | `json`, `csv`, `re`, `hashlib` (standard library) |\n| **Packaging & Trace Utils** | ✅ Ready | `/usr/bin/zip` for packaging `code.zip` and `agent-trace.zip` |\n\nNo further system or environment setup is required.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Translating This Challenge into C++ Terms\n\nComing from a C++ background, this problem becomes much clearer when framed around **systems programming**, **memory management**, and **state machines**.\n\n#### The Core Scenario: \"Memory Leak\" in a Distributed System\nIn C++, when you call `delete ptr;` or `free(ptr);`, if you fail to clean up secondary allocations, weak pointers, cache entries, or background workers holding references, you get:\n- **Use-After-Free / Memory Leaks** (data remains allocated in secondary memory).\n- **Dangling Pointers / Ghost Data** (someone dereferences old data that should have been gone).\n\nIn this challenge, Northstar Shop ran a distributed deletion routine (`delete_user(user_id)`). The workflow marked the task as `reported_status = COMPLETE`. But in reality:\n- A read-replica database still holds the user's row.\n- An analytics table still has the user's email un-anonymized.\n- A background synchronization worker (`connector`) is still active and copies deleted profiles back into the search index.\n- A backup archive has `restore_allowed = 1`, which could overwrite live storage with old customer data on the next recovery drill.\n\n**Your role is to write a Valgrind / MemorySanitizer for personal data.** Given a snapshot of the distributed system at timestamp `t = \n<truncated 1428 bytes>\nzer (Serialize ordered steps: FINDING -> REMEDIATION -> VERIFICATION)\n```\n\n1. **Input Classification (`request_text`)**:\n   - Filter out non-deletion requests. If the user asked *\"I'd like to download my data\"* or *\"Please update my phone number\"*, return `NOT_APPLICABLE, []`.\n2. **Pointer Aliasing (`user_identities.csv` & `user_audit_log.csv`)**:\n   - A user may have changed their email 6 months ago. The deletion request used their old email.\n   - We resolve the alias: `request_email` $\\to$ canonical `user_id` $\\to$ set of all current and past `{emails, phones, hashes, names}`.\n3. **Temporal Slicing (`captured_at <= audit_at`)**:\n   - Strict causality rule: Ignore any log, database snapshot, or document captured *after* the case's `audit_at` timestamp.\n4. **Invariant Checking (`deletion_rules.csv`)**:\n   - For every resource associated with the user across the 8 systems (`account_service`, `search_service`, `analytics_platform`, etc.):\n     - Is the profile deleted or tombstoned without PII?\n     - Is the search index entry deleted?\n     - Are analytics fields nullified (anonymized)?\n     - Are sync connectors disabled (`enabled == 0`)?\n     - Are backup restores blocked (`restore_allowed == 0`)?\n     - Are mandatory security audit logs retained (`PRESENT`)?\n5. **Deterministic Remediation Sequence**:\n   - If leaks exist:\n     - First, disable data flows & block restores (prevent race conditions / writes).\n     - Next, delete, redact, or anonymize remaining records.\n     - Finally, verify the resulting absent/redacted state.\n\n---\n\n### How We Will Build It\n\nWe will implement this in Python inside [`code/main.py`](file:///home/skandan-c-y/PycharmProjects/hackerrank-orchestrate-october26/code/main.py) because Python has built-in JSON, CSV, and SQLite engines to query the SQL snapshot dumps in memory with just a few lines of code.\n\nWe can validate our logic against all 32 known ground-truth cases in `sample_output.csv` to ensure 100% accuracy before executing the full 518 cases.\n\nReady to start building the pipeline?","truncated_fields":["content"]}
